.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Node\fP< dim >::Assign (uint32_t pnode, \fBElement\fP< dim > *const parent_elmt)"

.PP
assign new parent element where there is a NOT_INITIALISED slot in the parent element storage, else adds new one Assigns parent element pointer and remembers which node (local idx) of the parent element this node is\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIpnode\fP is the node number in the parent element of the assigned node\&. 
.br
\fIelement\fP is pointer to the parent element that gets assigned\&.
.RE
.PP
.SH "Input Arguments"
.PP
The local number of the parent element which is going to be assigned\&. 
.br
 The local node number of this node in the parent element\&. 
.br
 A pointer to the parent \fBElement\fP which shall be added\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> uint32_t \fBcsmp::Node\fP< dim >::AssignNodeNeighbors ()"

.PP
builds the neighbor connectivity working through higher-dimensional parent element edges that the node is part of; returns new number of neighbors Re-establishes the node neighbors of the node using the parent element connectivity\&. Since lower-dimensional elements share the nodes with the higher dimensional ones, they are ignored\&. Only corner nodes are considered in the first pass\&. Subsequently midside nodes which exist in higher-order elements are detected and their neighbors are assigned\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::Node\fP< dim >::AtBoundary (\fBBOX_BOUNDARY\fP b)"

.PP
flagging for box-shaped models: NOT, LEFT, BOTTOM, RIGHT, TOP, BACK, FRONT etc\&. Set or return the global boundary flag of the \fBNode\fP\&. 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> bool \fBcsmp::Node\fP< dim >::IsParent (const \fBElement\fP< dim > * const eptr) const"

.PP
checks whether \fBElement\fP is a parent of the node; call SortParents() first !!! checks whether \fBElement\fP is a parent of the node 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> \fBNode\fP< dim > * \fBcsmp::Node\fP< dim >::Neighbor (uint32_t neighbor_node) const"

.PP
access to any of the neighbor nodes Implements node connectivity graph, giving access to all the \fBNode\fP objects that the \fBNode\fP is connected to via \fBElement\fP, \fBFace\fP or \fBInterFace\fP edges\&. \fBNode\fP neighbors are enlisted in an order that is determined by sorting the points to them (this makes the neighbor vector searchable)\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIneighbor_node\fP the nth \fBNode\fP in the sortes vector of pointers to nodes stored in the node\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
If the node is a manifold (topologically co-located with other nodes that can be accessed looping over the branches of the manifold, then only those Nodes are neighbors who are on the same contiguous mesh patch as the \fBNode\fP, i\&.e\&., not shared with the other nodes in the manifold\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
SKM 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
8/10/2021 
.RE
.PP

.SS "template<uint32_t dim> uint32_t \fBcsmp::Node\fP< dim >::Neighbors () const"

.PP
the number of corner nodes that this node is directly connected with via segments If the parent elements are of the same dimension as the node, then there are exactly as many elements as are nodes\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
calculation is costly, compute only once per node loop!
.RE
.PP
\fBTest\fP
.RS 4
correct SKM (unit test exists) 
.RE
.PP

